hangs and sure we can call  that hypergravity training if you want I  definitely want hey Alan what have you  been up to today oh nothing you know  just the usual just hypergravity  training likewise you can also use these  kinds of grips these open hands two  fingers path crimps whilst you're doing  shrugs wants to do farmers walk of  course with a lighter weight but it's  another great way to challenge yourself  you can use this as incidental training  just whilst you're going about your  business carrying lots of shopping for  instance why not carry the bags from two  fingers and train your grip at the same  time likewise there's lots of other  kinds of grips you can use for instance  I talked about the pinch grip when I was  doing my talk on loaded carries and this  basically means you're grabbing two  weight plates or whatever and you pinch  them together with your hands and this  requires you to use a lot of force to  apply a lot of force with your fingers  in order to hold it and carry it or curl  it if you're doing weight plate curls  again there's lots of ways you can  combine your  grip strength training with other kinds  of training just to make sure that  you're getting it in there and another  way to train your fingers of course is  with finger push-ups and this is another  one you need to be very careful with but  this is what gyasi Raj demonstrated when  they asked him how he was able to do  this amazing climbing he'd got on his  hands and feet and started doing just  two finger push-ups or whatever  demonstrating how powerful his fingers  were Bruce Lee of course was famous of  doing two finger push-ups again I can't  do two finger push-ups I don't recommend  you try it you have to build up to it  you can do open hand push-ups on your  just all your fingertips to begin with  and then you can build up by removing  fingers do eight do six take one hand  away another way to build up to it is to  have one hand closed and one hand on  your fingertips if you can't do  fingertip press ups at all to begin with  that's a good progression to use and of  course all of those overcoming isometric  exercises I've talked about in the past  are also fantastic for grip strength so  these are all your things like trying to  rip a rope in half or bend a piece of  steel or rip up frying pan up or a  telephone book anything when you're  trying to push or pull something that  won't budge grip strength and isometrics  go hand in hand no pun intended  very well and not only that but you'll  be building the mind muscle connection  greatly it's great for general central  nervous system training and it'll build  the toughness on your skin you know your  calloused hands that help to improve  your grip because you know your hands  are grippy and also reduce the pain that  you get from doing anything that  involves a lot of grip but all of this  stuff will do that as well doing general  power lifting anything like rack pulls  or I use a hand dynamometer and this not  only allows them to train my grip but  also measure how much force I'm able to  generate and this is something very  interesting because apparently looking  at your grip strength is a very useful  correlate for your general central  nervous system recovery so if you have a  very weak grip in the morning it might  suggest that you have a bad cortisol to  testosterone ratio some athletes and  some trainers will use that as a cue to  say don't train that date you're not  feeding your best you're likely to  injure yourself so this again  demonstrates just how key grip strength  is to overall strength and of course  that's also why it's so important that  you be careful with this do you  introduce this kind of training slowly  because not only is there the risk of a  cue  injury you can also fatigue yourself  overtraining yourself  wear out your nervous system and that's  when you start to see detrimental  effects in other areas of your training  which is not what we want so instead of  doing dead hangs to failure multiple  times in each workout go to 50% 70% at  the end of your regular pull-up workout  like I say maybe once every other week  do wrist curls at the start of your last  training or your biceps training - this  just involve your forearms more try  going up on your fingers the next time  you do press ups etc be gentle with it  be sensible and build it up that way  there you go that's how you build  awesome grip strength awesome big  forearms so that you can climb so that  you can fight so that all of your lifts  become better  if you found this video useful and  interesting then please leave a like  please comment down below let me know  what you think if I've missed anything  out please share it around it helps me  immensely and subscribe to the channel  of course if you want to see more like  this I've got lots more on the way next  week I'm gonna be covering the next part  of my flow States video there's more  videos coming on fitness on bodybuilding  on productivity working online brain  training nootropics all that stuff so if  that sounds good then thanks a ton for  watching and I'll see you next time bye  for now
 

